WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.288 --> 00:00:08.610
- Okay, welcome Yang-Yang Zhou.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.640
And thank you everyone for
joining online and in-person.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.160
I'm so excited to be
introducing Yang-Yang.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.320
She's an assistant professor
of Political Science

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.690
at the University of British Columbia,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.340
currently at Harvard.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.087
And she's here to
present her book project,

00:00:22.087 --> 00:00:25.447
"Rejecting Coethnicity: The
Politics of Migrant Exclusion

00:00:25.447 --> 00:00:27.450
"by Minoritized Citizens."

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.850
So this is part of Yang-Yang's
broader research agenda

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.590
on the causes and consequences

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.960
of migration and forced displacement

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.930
with a focus on social cohesion outcomes

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.170
and looking at how shared
ethnicity can mediate

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.730
some of the backlash effects
that we find from migration.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.500
She also has another strand of her work

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.440
that looks specifically at
developing statistical tools

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.300
for asking sensitive
questions in sensitive context

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.550
and respecting respondent privacy.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.980
So I'm really excited to see

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.510
some of the fruits of this labor

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.340
on both strands of research potentially.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.053
And, yeah, join me in welcoming Yang-Yang.

00:01:05.053 --> 00:01:07.050
(audience applauding)

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:08.250
- Thanks so much.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.806
Thanks so much for having me.

00:01:09.806 --> 00:01:13.585
I'm happy to take questions
throughout the presentation,

00:01:13.585 --> 00:01:16.113
so please interrupt me.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.930
All right, thank you for
that very kind introduction.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.240
So I'm gonna start off this presentation

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.310
with a little bit of
audience participation,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.643
so I'm gonna need you to chime in.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.530
So we all have many
parts of our identities

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.850
that are important to us

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.280
and some of those identities
feel more important to us

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.533
compared to others at any given moment.

00:01:41.426 --> 00:01:44.490
Here are some possible
identity categories.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.200
They're not comprehensive.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.510
Maybe something like
your political identity,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:52.170
your race or ethnicity, your
nationality, your gender,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.693
your job, or maybe you just
feel uniquely me at the moment,

00:01:56.693 --> 00:02:01.260
not really feeling any
of these categories.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.430
If I ask you to imagine you
have like 10 identity tokens

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.310
that you can distribute
across these six categories,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.800
just asking you how would you do that

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.390
currently as you identify.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.340
So just take a moment and think about it.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.530
You don't have to tell me,
but just think about it.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.830
Where would you put more
of your identity tokens

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.000
into the different categories

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.973
because you feel more
strongly about that right now.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.323
Okay, I hope you had a moment
to think about it a little.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.820
And now my question is
what contextual change

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.880
would shift how you just
made that distribution?

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.370
Maybe you're in a different
place geographically,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.640
maybe other people are here,
you're in a different setting,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.210
or the setting changes around you.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.570
Any examples of how
this whole distribution

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:52.620
would shift for you

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.553
if you had a different
change in your context?

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:00.543
Yes, Salma.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.920
- [Salma] Well, race or
ethnicities would be big for me.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.940
Maybe five out of my 10
tokens would go there.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.280
- Okay.
- But if I was

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.950
in the Middle East, it would be less,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.330
whereas when I'm here, it's higher.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:13.770
- Why is that?

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:14.790
- [Salma] Because here I'm a minority

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.530
so it's more salient that I'm different,

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.450
but when I'm back home, it's not salient

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.860
'cause everyone's the same as me.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:20.730
- Interesting.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.700
Yeah, so what makes you
stand out in a context?

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.153
Sometimes you feel more
identified to that.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:30.773
Sarah.

00:03:30.773 --> 00:03:33.130
- [Sarah] Sure, yeah, I mean I think I put

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.720
five of my tokens in nationality

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.001
and that was probably
because I was recent,

00:03:39.001 --> 00:03:42.960
I'm traveling on spring break

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.240
and I just went through
these application processes,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.860
and so that's in my mind.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.410
- Yeah, of course, so you just
went through a whole process

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.863
that really heightened
your national identity.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.940
Thank you so much for sharing.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.630
I'm Chinese-American, and
so yeah, kind of the same,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:04.230
like when I'm here in the
U.S. I feel very Chinese.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.210
I think people, when they see me,

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.620
they just assume that I'm Chinese

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.770
and will try talking to me in Chinese.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.990
But when I'm in China I feel very American

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:14.887
because all the Chinese
people around me are like,

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:17.097
"Why is your Chinese so bad?"

00:04:18.037 --> 00:04:22.290
"Why don't you understand any
of our cultural references?"

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.280
Things like that.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.373
And so, but if my family were here,

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.600
I don't know, I think I
would feel more Chinese.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.660
So lots of different ways
that our context can change

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.130
and it can be very quick.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.280
So just a small shift can really shift

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.963
how strongly we're identifying.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:43.230
Right, thank you so much

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:47.310
for doing that little exercise with me.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.760
So basically the big research
question I'm trying to ask

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.130
in this project is what happens

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.260
when a new group of people arrive?

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.090
How does that shift the identities

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.430
of the existing group of people there?

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:03.720
And specifically I'm talking
about migration and migrants.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.140
So how do the existing
people, host citizens,

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.690
react when migrants come in,

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.570
and, in fact, they share similar cultural,

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.850
language, and ethnic backgrounds.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.500
So those host citizens, I'm
gonna call them coethnics

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.660
because they share ethnic
ties with the new migrants.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:25.683
And so if these host citizens
are not welcoming, why not?

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.280
And so a lot of the research
that's already been done

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.280
on immigration and immigrant reception

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.020
has been done in the U.S. and Europe,

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:41.010
and in these contexts, we know
that at least in the U.S.,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.690
we're really looking at migration

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.250
coming from Asian countries,

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.680
from Latin America or in Europe.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.930
It's migration coming from the Middle East

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.000
or North Africa for the most part.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.340
And there there are a lot of religious,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.530
racial, ethnic differences.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.190
And so these scholars and
many others have found that

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:03.180
when they're looking at
white majoritized citizens,

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.540
their antipathy towards immigrants,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:07.920
the new immigrants coming in,

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.010
a lot of that is driven
by fears over racial,

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.020
ethnic, religious differences.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.220
And so from that literature,

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.400
we might assume that
absent those differences,

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.930
if there's coethnicity,

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.830
there's gonna be a lot more welcoming.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.320
Similarly, we know from a lot
of sociological literature

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.600
that ethnic enclaves in a place,

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.200
so having host citizens
that share coethnic ties

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.240
is really important for
migrant integration.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.940
If you're a new migrant,
you go to a country,

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.430
you try sometimes to find
people who look like you,

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.140
who share your cultural background,

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.690
they tend to be the ones who
will have networks for you.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:43.860
You can find a dentist,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.330
you can get your kids enrolled in school.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:47.250
So those are really important.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:50.730
And we also know from a lot
of research done in the U.S.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:55.730
that if those groups are
minoritized in that country,

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.780
discrimination might foster

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.330
a sense of linked fate, of solidarity.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.360
So all of this research
points to your coethnics

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.000
should accept you, they
should welcome you.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.120
But of course we do see
cases of coethnic rejection

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.170
and a lot of that literature

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:11.880
is based on economic competition.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:15.120
So maybe these new migrants
come in, they look like you,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.330
importantly, they might share
the same set of skills as you.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:20.550
And so you might feel
economically competitive,

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.013
and that's why we see coethnic rejection.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.800
And policy makers largely
have this assumption too

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.340
that if there's coethnicity,
there's going to be welcoming.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:34.200
And so this is a rather long quote

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.990
from a senior UNHCR official.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:40.860
The UNHCR is the UN Agency on Refugees.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.400
And when I talked to this person, he said,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.070
refugees for the most
part cross the border

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.170
not too far from where they come from.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:51.510
These borders, especially
in Sub-Saharan Africa,

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:52.343
are artificial.

00:07:52.343 --> 00:07:54.510
I'll talk about what that
means in a little bit.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.880
But basically you have on
both sides of the border,

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.030
populations that know each
other and from the same clan.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:03.960
And the example he gives
is Dadaab camp in Kenya

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.370
with Somalis on either side,

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:07.560
which is the case I'm gonna talk about.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.270
He says these communities know each other,

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:10.774
they're homogenous,

00:08:10.774 --> 00:08:12.300
In this kind of context,

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.330
migrants don't go where
they're not welcomed or wanted.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:17.010
And so you move to parts of the region

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.293
where you know people will not reject you.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.480
And this is important because
the context he's talking about

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:27.660
is people who are migrating

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.270
because they're experiencing
some form of displacement

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.330
either through conflict
or natural disaster.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.360
And we know that these people,
the number of these people

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.340
are growing every year around the world,

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.700
but they're mostly staying
in Global South context.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:44.820
So they're not really
going to U.S. or Europe.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.707
They're mostly, as that
UNHCR official said,

00:08:47.707 --> 00:08:49.497
"They're just crossing the border."

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.810
Here's another way to look at this data.

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:59.810
My co-author Andrew Shaver and
I collected geo-located data

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:03.150
on where these displacement
populations are,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:08.150
like refugee camps, asylum
seeker, communities, IIDP camps.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:11.310
And again you can see most of them are,

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:13.770
a lot of them are in Sub-Saharan Africa

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:15.570
and other parts of the Global South.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:20.580
And so taking in that context,

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:25.580
my theory is that when the
host state and politicians

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.040
stigmatize migrants in a place,

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.290
those host citizens, the co-ethnics,

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:34.050
who share ties with those migrants,

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:35.730
they're gonna feel this fear

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:39.090
of being mistaken for
this stigmatized group.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.490
They're going to fear being migrantized

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:44.250
and discriminated against by the state

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:47.280
and by other majoritized
pop host citizens.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.330
So they're co-nationals

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.940
who belong to majority ethnic groups.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:52.980
They're gonna heighten
their national identity

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:54.480
in order to distance themselves

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.100
because that's something
that they don't share

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:57.780
with the new migrant groups.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:01.050
And they may espouse
anti-migrant sentiments,

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.480
and support anti-migrant policies,

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.932
which is particularly
troubling because, again,

00:10:06.932 --> 00:10:08.340
when the migrants come in,

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.240
they're looking towards these
communities to welcome them.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.870
And so very quickly, this is sort of like

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.643
how I lay out my cases.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.080
Here we can see these
two states of the world

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:23.160
either the host government

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.560
is stigmatizing migrants or they're not.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.290
When they do and you have coethnic groups

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.710
who are located kind of proximate to them,

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.450
in this case geographically.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.070
That's going to follow the case

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:36.690
where they're going to
reject those migrants

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.460
because they're gonna fear

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.721
being mistaken for migrant themselves.

00:10:40.721 --> 00:10:42.420
When they're farther away,

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.550
maybe they feel less of that pressure

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:47.160
and so they might react more neutrally.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.850
And then for your noncoethnic,
your majoritized groups,

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.590
they usually just take in

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:55.980
or help perpetuate some of
these negative stereotypes,

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:58.470
so they're gonna act exclusionary.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:10:59.737
Whereas in the case where the state

00:10:59.737 --> 00:11:02.100
does not stigmatize migrants,

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:04.200
even if you are mistaken
for being a migrant,

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.750
you're not going to really feel

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.730
like you're suffering
any negative consequences

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:09.720
for the most part,

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.780
and so here is where
we will see solidarity.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.683
These are the cases where,
yeah, coethnics, are welcoming.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.120
Okay, so I'm going to test this theory

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.020
mostly in the East African context,

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.030
but at the end I'll talk
about some other contexts

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.700
where I think this phenomenon happens,

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.133
which is a lot of places in the world.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.910
And the three countries I'm gonna focus on

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.150
are Tanzania, Kenya, and Uganda.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.585
Here's a map of all East Africa,

00:11:40.585 --> 00:11:43.740
you can see that these three countries,

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:48.369
they host a lot of refugees
and from neighboring countries,

00:11:48.369 --> 00:11:51.690
so a lot of groups are shared.

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:56.160
So they all host like
South Sudanese for example.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:59.160
And they've been hosting
refugees for a very long time.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.401
In fact, when the UNHCR starts recording,

00:12:01.401 --> 00:12:04.380
how many numbers of refugees
are in these countries,

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.510
they basically start at
the year of independence.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.190
So even at the year of independence,

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:09.540
there are refugees there,

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:10.950
meaning that there have
been refugees there

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:14.163
before independence and they
only start recording it then.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:19.440
And, yeah, the refugees

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.870
that they are hosting across the border,

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:25.620
they all tend to be
crossing colonial borders.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:28.050
And that means a lot of these borders

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.090
when you hear artificial colonial borders,

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:31.770
it's because colonial authorities

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.833
in the late 1800s, early 1900s,

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:38.070
in Western Europe drew these lines.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.520
So they're not drawn by
indigenous groups living there.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:44.250
And so what that mostly means today is

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:45.900
you can cross some of these borders

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:48.930
and you won't see anything,
you won't see the line.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.030
And communities across these borders

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:54.033
kind of just live as one
especially if they're coethnic.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:13:01.050
And so this is how the
cases in East Africa

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:02.880
sort of fit my theory.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:04.740
I'm gonna go through them line by line,

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.850
but let's start with the
Ha Tanzanians in Kigoma.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:12.120
And so this is a case where the Tanzanians

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.610
in the host country of Tanzania,

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:17.430
they are coethnic with
the Burundian refugees

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.440
coming in from the other
side of the border,

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.270
but they're living in a context

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.483
where the host government
stigmatizes refugees.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:33.240
And so Tanzania has been hosting

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.880
again refugees since independence,

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:38.757
but for throughout the 2000s,

00:13:38.757 --> 00:13:42.150
I think they were hosting
about like not too many,

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:44.730
maybe around 50,000 Congolese refugees

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.196
who've been there since the
90s, and that was pretty stable.

00:13:47.196 --> 00:13:49.830
And around that time up until 2014,

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.320
there was only one
refugee camp in Tanzania,

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.060
it's called Nyarugusu.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.580
And here's what it looked like in 2014

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:57.900
when you're there on the ground,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:01.020
they looked like
semi-permanent structures.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:03.570
They have their own blocks, their schools,

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:07.223
and, yeah, it's mostly Congolese refugees.

00:14:07.223 --> 00:14:10.500
In early 2015 there was a political crisis

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:12.330
in the neighboring country of Burundi.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.150
The president there wanted
to change the constitution

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:18.420
and run for a third term that
kicked off a lot of protests.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:22.216
And so a lot of people
especially thinking about

00:14:22.216 --> 00:14:24.376
the genocide there that just happened

00:14:24.376 --> 00:14:29.010
like a few years earlier,
they went to Tanzania,

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:32.070
and here you can see that the refugee camp

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:35.643
basically in a few months'
time, more than doubled in size.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:41.070
And I think it was about
like 230,000 refugees

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:42.060
from Burundi came.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:46.627
And so this area is hosting
like 50,000, here it's like 230.

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:49.263
And so what that looks like here,

00:14:49.263 --> 00:14:51.060
it's not so many permanent structure,

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:54.303
it's just rows and rows of UNHCR tents.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:03.300
And this is a context where
the host government of Tanzania

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.610
represses the Burundian refugees.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:07.080
So they force them to be in camps.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.900
They're not allowed to leave the camps.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:11.910
Recently they have been pressuring them

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:13.530
to return to Burundi.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.990
They've been forcibly
disappearing and torturing them.

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:19.020
And then the rhetoric
around Burundian refugees

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.110
is one of criminality, security concerns,

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.303
disease concerns, overcrowding.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.480
And so to give you a sense,
here is the country of Tanzania.

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:33.120
These are where the sort of economic

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:35.640
and political capitals are in the country,

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:37.950
and the region that we're
interested in is Kigoma.

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:38.940
It's all the way over here.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:42.702
It's a border region,
bordering Burundi right there.

00:15:42.702 --> 00:15:46.200
As a border region, it is poorer

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.110
compared to many of the
other regions in Tanzania.

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:50.790
Kigoma often ranks worse

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:54.000
in terms of like school
outcomes, health outcomes,

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.880
so it is a marginalized area.

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.718
In Kigoma, the majority
group in that region

00:15:59.718 --> 00:16:01.110
is called the Ha,

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:04.710
but they are a minority group nationally.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.350
And, yeah, they are considered coethnic

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:09.505
with the Burundi who
choose on the other side,

00:16:09.505 --> 00:16:11.910
meaning they do speak the same language,

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:13.710
they have similar cultural backgrounds,

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:15.240
they often intermarry,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.750
and that sort of border in between the two

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:20.490
is kind of nonexistent.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:21.323
So even when I was there,

00:16:21.323 --> 00:16:24.630
it was really easy just
to walk across the border.

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:29.149
And communities on either side
use the same water sources,

00:16:29.149 --> 00:16:30.903
go to the same markets.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:35.670
And so this is zooming in
on that area of Kigoma.

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.650
Here is the sort of
regional capital Kigoma town

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:42.540
and these are the three refugees
camp that are there now.

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:46.800
So Nyarugusu was the one that
I showed you photos before.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.110
That was there sort of, yeah,

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:52.230
to receive all the new refugees in 2015.

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:53.670
At the end of 2016,

00:16:53.670 --> 00:16:55.620
there were so many new refugees coming in

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:57.750
that they opened up these two other camps,

00:16:57.750 --> 00:17:00.127
Nduta and Mtendeli.

00:17:02.252 --> 00:17:05.610
And so in 2015 just a few months

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:08.550
after the refugees started coming in,

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:12.570
I was there and I got to do
some community focus groups,

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:15.630
and so here I chose these sort
of 10 community focus groups.

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:19.440
I did, that sort of blue
dot is where Nyarugusu is,

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:21.630
and then these are like the
10 different communities

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.600
that I kind of randomly selected

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:25.980
to do community focus groups.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:30.120
Each focus group I think had
around like 10 to 15 people.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.950
And again these are all Tanzanians.

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:33.990
They're Ha Tanzanians.

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:35.190
And then the following summer

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:36.660
I came back and I ran a survey.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.720
So this shows you basically
where my survey respondents are.

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:40.553
To give you a sense

00:17:40.553 --> 00:17:41.720
there's like a lot of
geographic variation, right?

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:44.850
Some of the survey respondents
are quite close to the camp

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:45.843
and some are far.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:50.940
And again in the survey,

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.100
this is how I sort of measure identity.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:55.080
It's that same distributional measure

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:58.950
that I showed you before except
the categories are Kigoma,

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:02.070
that's like their subnational
regional identity,

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:06.390
your ethnic group, your
national identification,

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:10.650
your region of East Africa,
maybe you feel pan-African,

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:15.650
or you have this like sort of
catchall kind of last category

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:18.417
of I'm just me, I don't really
fit any of these things.

00:18:18.417 --> 00:18:20.946
And so in my survey I
basically gave them 10 tokens

00:18:20.946 --> 00:18:24.990
to distribute across these
six categories, yeah?

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.640
- [Audience Member] Just two
quick clarifying questions.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:29.130
So I'm wondering what your use of the word

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:29.963
ethnicity and coethnic.

00:18:29.963 --> 00:18:32.160
- Yeah.
- So the example you gave like

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:36.780
Ha Tanzanians with Tutsi Burundians.

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:38.430
So they're not the same ethnicity,

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:39.750
but they're considered coethnic.

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:42.960
So.
- Yeah, with the Hutu, yeah.

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:46.020
Yeah, I should have defined coethnicity.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:47.280
That's a great question.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:51.780
So coethnicity in my
case is sort of it's both

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:54.120
that you yourself have some sort of

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.130
recognition of coethnicity.

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:58.590
So based on like descent-based ties

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:02.760
or a myth of a common
origin, all of those things.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:04.740
So you have some agency over that.

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.720
And so those communities in Burundi,

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:07.680
I think they're called Hutu,

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:09.420
but in Tanzania they're called Ha,

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:12.483
but they do, they speak the
same language, they intermarry,

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:16.440
and so there there's like
the self-recognition part,

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:18.180
but I think the other
really interesting part

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:21.641
of defining coethnicity
is like your recognition

00:19:21.641 --> 00:19:25.860
that other people will group
you into the same group.

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:27.030
And so there's a part of that

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:28.740
where you don't have much control over,

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:31.230
but as like a second order concern,

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:32.227
you recognize that other people

00:19:32.227 --> 00:19:34.530
are going to say that we're coethnic

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:37.260
even if I might not
consider myself coethnic.

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:40.020
So I mean like with other East Asians,

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:43.440
like people coming from Japan or Korea,

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:45.794
like I might not say that we're coethnic,

00:19:45.794 --> 00:19:50.794
but I recognize that other
Americans would look at us

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:53.760
and say, oh yeah, you are a
part of the same ethnic group.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:54.593
- [Audience Member] So is it based

00:19:54.593 --> 00:19:56.070
on like physical descriptors?

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:59.400
- It could be language, yeah.

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:00.233
- [Audience Member] Okay.

00:20:00.233 --> 00:20:02.100
- Cultural markings, dress,

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:03.950
all the sort of things that we might,

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.310
trying to find ethnicity with.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:11.370
But I think it's just the
important part is like

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:13.590
part of it is my own self-recognition

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:15.930
and part of it is my
recognition of how others,

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:18.330
outsiders might group me.

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:20.340
- [Audience Member] And
why is religion not one

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:21.300
of the circles?

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:22.440
- Oh that's a great question.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:25.080
Okay, if I put religion in here,

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:27.603
the whole region is very Christian,

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:31.380
and it's the same across the border.

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.410
So if I had put in your religion,

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:37.080
I think everyone would
really just put that.

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:39.930
And so the categories
I'm really interested in

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:43.560
are obviously ethnic group
and your national identity.

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:44.880
So comparing ethnic and national.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:47.730
So I wanted to make sure
that the other categories

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:51.480
were kind of just not many
people would use those categories

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:53.430
so I could see this variation.

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:54.720
But, yeah, if I put in religion,

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:56.010
I think everyone would just

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:58.083
put all their tokens into religion.

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:01.743
But it is a very important identity.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:06.450
Okay, so the other part of the survey,

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:08.520
so that's how I measure
identity in the survey,

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:12.540
the other part was half
of the respondents,

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.390
and I should say there was about
like 2000 respondents here,

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:19.920
half of them just started the survey

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:22.380
without knowing it's a
survey about refugees.

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.210
They got those questions about identity,

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:25.200
they saw that measure.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:26.610
So they're going in with the survey

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:28.470
not knowing it's about refugees.

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:31.770
The other half start off with
questions about refugees.

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:33.900
So a little bit of information

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:36.170
like there's this many that just came in

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:38.970
in the last few months,
they live in Nyarugusu.

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:41.820
We show them a map of where Nyarugusu is

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.440
and then ask them some questions

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:45.840
like do you know which
countries they're from?

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.550
And some other factual questions.

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:49.170
So for them they're really primed

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:50.003
to be thinking about refugees

00:21:50.003 --> 00:21:51.420
'cause they just answered

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:53.010
a whole bunch of questions about them.

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:55.170
So that group I'm gonna
call the treatment group

00:21:55.170 --> 00:21:57.864
'cause they're primed to
be thinking about refugees.

00:21:57.864 --> 00:22:01.051
So in the control group,
again these people don't know

00:22:01.051 --> 00:22:03.180
it's a survey about refugees.

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:05.460
This is what those identities,

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.080
their ethnic identification

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:09.938
and their national
identification look like,

00:22:09.938 --> 00:22:12.720
the difference between coethnics,

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.850
the HA versus non coethnics.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:16.440
Now I will note that in this area,

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.583
there's very few non-coethnics.

00:22:17.583 --> 00:22:21.030
I think my coethnics are
like 90% of the sample.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:23.490
So there's just not that much
variation for non-coethnics.

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:27.030
But even still you can
see that for coethnic,

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:29.294
compared to non-coethnics,
they feel more ethnic.

00:22:29.294 --> 00:22:30.810
And that probably makes sense

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:33.483
because they are the ethnic
majority in the region.

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:38.160
But if we turn to the treatment effect,

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:42.600
so what is it for people
who actually answer

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:45.480
all those questions about refugees first?

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:50.130
We see there's no difference
now on ethnic identity,

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:53.013
but for your coethnics,
they feel more national.

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:56.400
And so so far it looks,

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:58.980
it's suggested that it's fitting my theory

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:02.703
that when people, when
coethnics are feeling like,

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:07.530
in this case, sort of
psychologically proximate to refugees

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:08.970
because they're being reminded of them,

00:23:08.970 --> 00:23:11.370
they're being reminded
that, hey, here's this group

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:14.010
that kind of shares our ethnic identity,

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:15.660
they end up feeling more national

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:18.906
because that's the thing
that differentiates them.

00:23:18.906 --> 00:23:21.150
We can look at this another way.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:24.450
So here on the x-axis,

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:27.570
we're looking at distance from the camp,

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:30.210
because, again, when we surveyed people,

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:32.880
I chose some people to be
quite far from the camp,

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:35.070
some people to be quite close.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:38.010
And so here this means you're
really close to the camp,

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:39.630
here you're much farther away.

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:41.970
And we can see this is just at baseline.

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:42.934
Again this is the control group

00:23:42.934 --> 00:23:45.353
who don't even know that
this is a question about,

00:23:45.353 --> 00:23:47.520
this is a survey about refugees.

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:49.380
For the people who are super close,

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:51.510
they also feel more national.

00:23:51.510 --> 00:23:53.489
And so this is just like
an observational component.

00:23:53.489 --> 00:23:55.920
Just by being close to the camp,

00:23:55.920 --> 00:23:57.480
they're gonna feel more national.

00:23:57.480 --> 00:23:58.313
Yeah?

00:23:58.313 --> 00:23:59.520
- [Audience Member] Just
to clarify a question

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:01.410
on the baseline,

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:06.410
do you also see that for
your larger treatment group,

00:24:06.780 --> 00:24:08.850
those who are closer to the camp,

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:12.270
do they answer those
questions about refugees

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:14.397
more accurately in terms of like

00:24:14.397 --> 00:24:18.060
knowing that their ethnicity
and like knowing where--

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:19.620
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:22.740
So for the control group,
they get those same questions,

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:24.450
they just get it at the end of the survey.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:28.800
So I do get measures for both
and there's no difference.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:30.810
I think maybe, no, wait,

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:32.100
there is a little bit of a difference.

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:32.933
The people who are closer

00:24:32.933 --> 00:24:35.567
know that there's definitely
Burundian refugees there.

00:24:35.567 --> 00:24:38.190
I think everyone knows that
there's Burundian refugees.

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.223
Maybe the people who are farthest away,

00:24:41.223 --> 00:24:43.260
I think there's some
percentile I looked at.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:45.720
They would also say there's
Rwanda's refugees there,

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:46.740
and there aren't.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:49.800
So I think maybe they do
have less information,

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:53.010
but everyone knows that there's
Burundian refugees there.

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:54.210
That's a great question.

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:58.020
And here I'm gonna show you that marginal,

00:24:58.020 --> 00:24:59.160
that additional effect

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:01.650
of getting the survey
treatment by distance.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:05.340
And you can see that for people

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:08.010
who are closest to the camp,

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:09.420
when you get that reminder

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:11.160
that the survey is about refugees,

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:13.320
they also feel more national,

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:14.515
and that effect sort of dissipates

00:25:14.515 --> 00:25:16.863
as you get farther and farther away.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:19.443
- [Audience Member] In terms
of the social networks,

00:25:19.443 --> 00:25:21.630
like are there social
networks that connect

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:22.799
the two sides together?

00:25:22.799 --> 00:25:26.670
Like I'm thinking about Syrian refugees

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:28.050
that come to the U.S.,

00:25:28.050 --> 00:25:30.641
like they very quickly
tap into like the area--

00:25:30.641 --> 00:25:31.474
- Yes.
- of the Muslim

00:25:31.474 --> 00:25:32.524
communities here and they help them

00:25:32.524 --> 00:25:33.990
with resources and whatever.

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:34.912
So is there like any network happening?

00:25:34.912 --> 00:25:36.540
Or are they just like,
- Yes.

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:37.380
- [Audience Member] Oh, we're not them.

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:38.213
They're a whole different--

00:25:38.213 --> 00:25:39.690
- That is a great question.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:41.610
So the next part of this,

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.601
I'm gonna talk about focus groups,

00:25:43.601 --> 00:25:46.830
but before I get into
focus groups' results,

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:48.150
the focus groups themselves

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:50.610
I think shed a lot of light into this.

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:53.443
One focus group I went
to was at the border.

00:25:53.443 --> 00:25:58.443
I sort of start off my book
introduction with the story.

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:02.340
When I went I sort of went
to ask these questions

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:04.200
like what do you think about the people

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.450
on this other side of the border?

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:08.670
What do you think about refugees?

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:10.110
Tell me what it means to be Tanzanian.

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.300
Those were the kinds of questions I asked.

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:16.410
And there was like a
village elder there and he,

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:17.441
the whole sort of focus group,

00:26:17.441 --> 00:26:20.790
the other community members
started turning on him,

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:21.727
and they were saying,

00:26:21.727 --> 00:26:23.257
"Oh that's our village elder"

00:26:23.257 --> 00:26:25.717
"and he hides refugees in his house."

00:26:25.717 --> 00:26:27.487
"Like they're there right now if you go,"

00:26:27.487 --> 00:26:28.837
"like they're supposed to be in the camp,"

00:26:28.837 --> 00:26:30.330
"but he's hiding them.""

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:33.450
And they said that like
there were sex workers

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:36.510
and he was involved in
like criminal activity.

00:26:36.510 --> 00:26:39.060
And we basically had to kinda
change the topic a little

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:40.860
'cause it was getting really heated.

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:43.773
And at the end, I approached
him, and I was like,

00:26:44.797 --> 00:26:49.797
"I'm very sorry, like do you
wanna tell me anything or?"

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:54.390
And he said that he was hosting
his niece who is Burundian

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:55.740
on the other side of the border,

00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:57.660
and he's like, yeah, they're my family,

00:26:57.660 --> 00:26:59.220
yeah, they're staying with me.

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:04.220
But it actually ended
up causing a lot of like

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:06.000
mistrust throughout the community,

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.790
and a lot of the community
members are trying to like,

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:11.580
yeah, go to immigration
officials to tell on him

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:15.390
so that a bus can come
and take those Burundians,

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:17.850
his family members, to the camp,

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:19.871
because they're supposed
to be in the camp.

00:27:19.871 --> 00:27:22.681
Yeah, so, and there was a lot of rumors

00:27:22.681 --> 00:27:26.490
about people hiding
refugees in their houses,

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:31.490
because, in a lot of cases,
they're not just like coethnics,

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:35.564
but they're kin, they're
literal relatives.

00:27:35.564 --> 00:27:38.183
- [Audience Member] And
what is the composition

00:27:38.183 --> 00:27:41.880
of both the control and
the treatment group?

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:44.338
I mean do you have like a good mixture of,

00:27:44.338 --> 00:27:46.066
I don't know, gender and age

00:27:46.066 --> 00:27:47.520
and other
- Yeah.

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:49.440
- [Audience Member]
Socioeconomic environment.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:51.780
- So for the treatment
groups, it's randomized.

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:54.750
So it is, because I have
like 2,000 respondents,

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:57.273
it is very balanced on
all of those things.

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:00.180
And just in my sample as a whole,

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:04.173
I have practically half men and women.

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:08.190
But the thing that is imbalanced though

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:10.350
is of course like distance to the camp.

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:13.680
So people who are closer have
different characteristics

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:15.690
than people who are farther away.

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:18.000
- [Audience Member] And
the results that you get,

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.940
that you have shown so far,

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:24.843
do they still hold depending
on these other characteristics?

00:28:24.843 --> 00:28:26.430
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:29.490
So one way we can do that is
just covariate adjustment.

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:31.620
Try and control for all the other

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:34.590
demographic characteristics
that we measure for.

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:37.200
But another way I try to get at that is,

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:38.370
if you recall in the map

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:39.990
that I showed you a few slides earlier,

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:42.030
there's actually two other camps

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:45.420
that at this point of the
survey they don't exist yet,

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:48.150
but a few, like almost a year later,

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:50.670
there's going to be two future camps,

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:55.620
and so I basically use them
as like a placebo test, yeah.

00:28:55.620 --> 00:28:57.780
And so I can look at this exact thing

00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:00.000
but it's at a distance to Nduta

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.750
or distance to Mtendeli camp,
and there's no results there.

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:05.730
And so we can think those future camps

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:07.950
kind of share the same characteristics

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:10.260
of like what makes it that refugees,

00:29:10.260 --> 00:29:11.560
that there's a camp there.

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:13.553
Good question.

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:18.570
Okay, so that was just
giving you an overview

00:29:18.570 --> 00:29:22.050
of how in this context, coethnics,

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:24.810
especially when they're
reminded that refugees are there

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:26.670
or that they're physically
close to refugees,

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:28.170
they feel more national.

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:30.510
But what does the sort of
national identity mean?

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:31.920
We know national identity

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:35.160
encompasses a lot of
different attitudes, feelings,

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:38.880
and so we can turn to sort
of the focus group evidence

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:40.930
to look at this a bit more qualitatively.

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:43.830
I only did 10 focus groups.

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:45.000
So just keep that in mind.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:46.980
It's a pretty small end.

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:48.570
But I sort of had the same approach

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:51.420
where I wanted to do
something experimental.

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:53.700
So on half of the groups,

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:56.730
randomly we would kinda do the same thing.

00:29:56.730 --> 00:29:59.580
We'd start off the
discussion with refugees,

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:02.250
and then we end the
discussion with what is it,

00:30:02.250 --> 00:30:04.770
what is your national
identity mean to you?

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:06.570
What does it mean to be Tanzanian?

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:08.550
And so those are five groups

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:11.250
and, again, each group has
a mixture of men and women.

00:30:12.427 --> 00:30:14.053
And then in the no prime condition,

00:30:14.053 --> 00:30:16.620
that's the control condition,
we just flipped it.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:20.610
So we talk about national
identity, living in the border,

00:30:20.610 --> 00:30:23.160
sort of the issues with your community,

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:24.870
what does it mean to be Tanzanian first,

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:27.843
and then we talk about refugees, right?

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:33.760
And so here's a quote from
the no prime condition

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:38.400
when sort of living in
the border comes up,

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:40.267
this person says, "We have villages

00:30:40.267 --> 00:30:43.417
"on either side of the border
that use the same river.

00:30:43.417 --> 00:30:45.187
"Of course we know each other.

00:30:45.187 --> 00:30:48.480
"You will find a Burundian
with uncles in Tanzania."

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:51.723
So this is very much a story
of coethnicity and kinship.

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:55.560
- [Audience Member] So
you have five focus groups

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:56.393
in the prime condition

00:30:56.393 --> 00:30:57.660
and five in the non-prime.
- Yeah.

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:58.910
- [Audience Member] Okay.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:02.760
- And then in the prime condition,

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:04.530
when we talk about refugees first,

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:06.450
these are kind of the quotes that I see.

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:09.240
So here look, it's like,
"We know each other."

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:12.427
Here it's "Refugees will
try to hide in our villages.

00:31:12.427 --> 00:31:14.857
"But we Tanzanians know each other.

00:31:14.857 --> 00:31:16.860
"It's clear we're not refugees."

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:20.100
So here the conversation was
probably like how do you tell.

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:22.650
This is like a question
that I try to ask everyone,

00:31:22.650 --> 00:31:25.440
like is it easy to tell
who's Burundian or not?

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:29.400
Because like appearance, language,

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:31.263
all of these things are very similar.

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:33.607
This other person says,

00:31:33.607 --> 00:31:35.737
"The new refugees are not good people

00:31:35.737 --> 00:31:37.380
"because of their nature."

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:40.087
So they're already sort
of dehumanizing them.

00:31:40.087 --> 00:31:43.350
"To kill a person, they see it as normal."

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.813
So a lot of quotes that are distancing.

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:52.910
And so we can gather all of
these focus group sentiments.

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:57.510
I think there's comments,
around like 300 comments,

00:31:57.510 --> 00:32:00.390
and I had an RA basically code them

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:04.590
based on when they're talking
about national identity,

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:07.260
are they using in-group
terms or out-group terms?

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:09.270
Are they talking about
their national identity

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:11.883
as something that's
inherited or is it inclusive?

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:16.410
Do they want citizenship
to be restricted or open?

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:18.450
And in all of these you can
see the treatment group,

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:20.730
when we talk about refugees first,

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:24.293
there's a lot more
exclusionary nationalism

00:32:24.293 --> 00:32:26.643
and restrictions on citizenship.

00:32:29.550 --> 00:32:31.680
Okay, so that gives, I just picked out

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:34.350
sort of some bits of evidence
from the Tanzania case.

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:35.940
Moving on to Kenya very quickly,

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:38.240
I'm just gonna show you
one result from Kenya.

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:44.040
And the Kenya example is kind of similar.

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:46.950
They are in a context
where the Kenyan state

00:32:46.950 --> 00:32:49.470
does stigmatize migrants, refugees,

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:51.360
but really they mostly target one group

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:53.820
which are the Somali refugees.

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:55.860
And so there are Somali Kenyans

00:32:55.860 --> 00:33:00.690
that live near the main
Somali refugee camp Dadaab.

00:33:00.690 --> 00:33:03.930
And in this case, in the
previous case in Tanzania,

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:06.360
I don't have that many non-coethnics,

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:07.770
but in here I could do a survey

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:08.880
in another part of the country

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:13.410
where there are other
refugees, the South Sudanese,

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:15.210
and in that case, the people,

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:16.560
the host community living there,

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:18.690
they're not considered coethnic.

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:21.060
So they have different traditions,

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:23.730
they tell me that they look differently,

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:25.260
they dress differently.

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:26.287
They actually say like,

00:33:26.287 --> 00:33:30.157
"Oh, like the refugees
are much taller than us.

00:33:30.157 --> 00:33:31.507
"So when we see someone from far away,

00:33:31.507 --> 00:33:33.097
"we just know that, like just by looking,

00:33:33.097 --> 00:33:35.397
"we can tell that they're a refugee."

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:42.210
And in Kenya similarly, every
few years the Kenyan state

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:44.430
sort of threatens to
shut down all the camps

00:33:44.430 --> 00:33:46.980
and force people back to their countries.

00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:49.950
And here, there's different reports.

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:52.170
There's a couple done
by Human Rights Watch

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:56.310
that say the Kenyan police,

00:33:56.310 --> 00:33:59.310
they will go after refugees
who fall under curfews,

00:33:59.310 --> 00:34:01.800
and especially in times where
there's like terrorist attacks

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:03.870
that have happened in the past years.

00:34:03.870 --> 00:34:06.150
There's a lot of
restrictions around refugees,

00:34:06.150 --> 00:34:09.150
Somali refugees, whether
they can leave the camps.

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:12.420
And when they encounter Somali Kenyans,

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:16.140
so these are Kenyan citizens
of Somali ethnic descent,

00:34:16.140 --> 00:34:19.260
they will paint them with the same brush.

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:22.500
They will also arrest them,
put them under curfew,

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:24.067
and here's a police, quoting, saying,

00:34:24.067 --> 00:34:24.900
"You're all terrorists.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:26.997
"Like you're all the
same to me basically."

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:31.620
And so this past fall,

00:34:31.620 --> 00:34:34.320
I did a very similar
kinda survey experiment

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:36.390
in these two parts of Kenya.

00:34:36.390 --> 00:34:38.970
Dadaab, this is bordering Somalia,

00:34:38.970 --> 00:34:43.500
and so Dadaab hosts Somalia refugees.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:48.500
Kakuma camp hosts South Sudanese
refugees for the most part.

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:52.920
They're also in sort of border regions.

00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:55.020
So when we go, this is Kakuma camp,

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.180
it's like technically two camps,

00:34:57.180 --> 00:34:58.980
and these are my survey respondents there.

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:01.800
They're mostly of Turkana ethnicity,

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:03.450
so I'm gonna call them Turkana Kenyans.

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:05.100
And again they're not really considered

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:07.143
coethnic with the refugees near them.

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:12.450
This shows the three camps under Dadaab.

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:16.320
And in the purple are my
Somali Kenyan respondents

00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:18.177
and they are coethnic.

00:35:18.177 --> 00:35:20.490
And so just one quick result,

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:21.960
again when I use that sort of

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:24.300
distributional identity measure,

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:27.900
we can compare, this is just at baseline,

00:35:27.900 --> 00:35:29.160
ethnic identification

00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:31.260
and national identification.

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:33.330
Here we can see for both groups,

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:38.101
the Garissa is Somali Kenyans coethnic,

00:35:38.101 --> 00:35:41.683
these are non-coethnic, and
both are pretty similar.

00:35:41.683 --> 00:35:46.683
They feel pretty ethnic,
somewhat national but less so.

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:50.610
But under the survey, I had
two treatment conditions,

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:52.800
one I'm gonna call camp closure,

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:57.480
and this is sort of, I presented
it as a radio news story,

00:35:57.480 --> 00:35:59.580
where they heard a little radio snippet

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:02.370
about the Kenyan government is announcing

00:36:02.370 --> 00:36:06.300
that in the past wanted
to close all the camps

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:07.800
for different reasons.

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:11.700
One is like security concerns,
environmental concerns.

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:14.130
And so again, it's sort of is a,

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:16.890
hopefully like a nuanced
and not super heavy-handed,

00:36:16.890 --> 00:36:18.270
but it is a reminder

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:20.703
that refugees are
stigmatized in the country.

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:24.630
The other treatment is
called refugee rights.

00:36:24.630 --> 00:36:27.570
So Kenya is sort of like a
transitional stage right now

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.390
because last year they
passed new legislation

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:33.660
that would actually treat
refugees more inclusively.

00:36:33.660 --> 00:36:36.210
It's not being implemented
yet, but it is possible.

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:39.810
So some things like allowing
refugees to leave the camps,

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:41.940
allowing them the formal right to work.

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:45.300
So this treatment, they
heard a news story that was,

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:46.380
yeah, basically saying, hey,

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:48.630
refugees might be treated
better in this country,

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:50.400
they might be given new rights.

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:53.190
And then the baseline
was like a placebo story

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:54.993
about motorcycle taxis.

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:57.750
And what we can see is that

00:36:57.750 --> 00:37:01.920
under the sort of stigmatizing
news story camp closure,

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:05.560
for coethnics, again they feel less ethnic

00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:08.223
and they feel more national.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:11.130
Under the treatment where they're hearing

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:13.470
that refugees might be given more rights,

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:14.303
they actually,

00:37:14.303 --> 00:37:16.980
these effects are not
statistically significant,

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:17.813
but for the top one,

00:37:17.813 --> 00:37:20.560
you can see they actually
feel a little bit more ethnic.

00:37:21.630 --> 00:37:22.980
And again that's the identity

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:24.900
that they share with the refugees.

00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:28.170
For your non-coethnics,
just null results basically.

00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:29.848
And that's what we would expect.

00:37:29.848 --> 00:37:33.900
Their identification does
not hinge on how refugees

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:36.150
near them are being
treated for the most part,

00:37:36.150 --> 00:37:37.043
'cause they know they're never gonna be

00:37:37.043 --> 00:37:39.510
mistaken as a refugee.

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:43.890
And so in this same survey I
wanted to ask more explicitly,

00:37:43.890 --> 00:37:46.740
do you ever get
misidentified as a refugee?

00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:48.960
Does your family ever get misidentified?

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:51.630
If so, how upset would you feel?

00:37:51.630 --> 00:37:56.070
And if you've been misidentified,
who misidentified you?

00:37:56.070 --> 00:37:58.315
And so you can select from
different groups of people.

00:37:58.315 --> 00:38:01.950
And of course Somali Kenyans
were way more likely to say

00:38:01.950 --> 00:38:03.240
that they have been misidentified.

00:38:03.240 --> 00:38:05.940
I think one out of four
of my survey respondents

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:07.740
said that they were misidentified.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:12.610
And for them, so for the 25%

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:14.790
that say they've been misidentified,

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:18.150
when I ask who, it's
overwhelmingly the police

00:38:18.150 --> 00:38:20.850
and it's overwhelmingly
other government officials.

00:38:20.850 --> 00:38:23.100
So it's the state who misidentifies them.

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:24.900
And when that happens,
it's really consequential,

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:27.300
because that means they're
gonna be put under curfew,

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:29.550
they might be jailed, detained,

00:38:29.550 --> 00:38:31.833
asked to see their
papers, things like that.

00:38:34.614 --> 00:38:39.150
All right, so lastly this
is the final case of Uganda

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:41.070
where it's kind of like my control case

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:43.233
in which there is coethnicity,

00:38:44.250 --> 00:38:46.200
but they're living in a context

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:48.300
where the Ugandan state does not,

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:49.470
although it's authoritarian,

00:38:49.470 --> 00:38:52.230
it does not stigmatize the refugees there.

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:54.300
In fact, politicians often talk

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:56.010
and newspapers talk about refugees

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:57.813
as if they are brothers and sisters.

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:02.280
And so in this case I
would expect solidarity.

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:04.200
And so here's some examples.

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:06.180
A lot of newspaper examples saying

00:39:06.180 --> 00:39:10.683
like "Uganda stands out
in refugee hospitality.

00:39:11.677 --> 00:39:14.407
"They're a model of how to treat refugees.

00:39:14.407 --> 00:39:16.410
"Refugees are like our brothers."

00:39:16.410 --> 00:39:19.593
That's often the sort of
elite rhetoric that we see.

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:23.910
Of course this doesn't mean
there isn't any social conflict

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:27.573
between host communities
and the refugees there,

00:39:28.680 --> 00:39:30.702
but I think there's just a lot less

00:39:30.702 --> 00:39:34.290
of what we should see
in those other cases.

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:36.720
And so this fall, I have some funding.

00:39:36.720 --> 00:39:41.720
I'm gonna do a very similar
survey in this part of Uganda.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:45.030
It's like the sort of northwestern part.

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:48.240
This is one of the largest
refugee camps in the world.

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:50.700
It's called Bidi Bidi and
it hosts South Sudanese.

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:52.080
And the host communities around there

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:54.420
I'm expecting are going to be coethnic.

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:57.600
You can already see here, so
this is Kampala, the capital,

00:39:57.600 --> 00:40:00.090
and sort of where all the refugees are.

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:01.404
You can already see here that

00:40:01.404 --> 00:40:04.140
in Tanzania it was like three camps,

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:08.310
in Kenya it's two camps, here
there's over 30 settlements.

00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:10.140
And that's because refugees
are allowed to live

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.240
where they want to for the most part.

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:13.680
They're not forced to be in camps.

00:40:13.680 --> 00:40:16.195
So here they just sort of naturally settle

00:40:16.195 --> 00:40:19.113
all along the western part of the country.

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:24.093
All right, so in conclusion,

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:28.290
I think in this project I
really wanna emphasize that

00:40:28.290 --> 00:40:31.950
hosting policies, political rhetoric,

00:40:31.950 --> 00:40:35.160
institution structures
can shape the relationship

00:40:35.160 --> 00:40:36.930
between citizens and migrants.

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:38.689
A lot of the immigration literature we see

00:40:38.689 --> 00:40:40.530
asks about public opinion

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:43.080
of how citizens feel towards immigrants

00:40:43.080 --> 00:40:48.080
and it almost assumes that
the context is a given.

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:50.250
So do you oppose migrants

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:52.530
because you feel economically competitive

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:54.612
or racial discrimination?

00:40:54.612 --> 00:40:57.030
But what I'm saying is that
there's structures in place

00:40:57.030 --> 00:40:58.780
that can really shape those things.

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:03.210
Second, when we do see instances

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:06.120
of anti-migrant policies and rhetoric,

00:41:06.120 --> 00:41:08.550
they're almost in a way doubly pernicious.

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:09.600
They're pernicious first

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:12.120
because they teach majoritized citizens

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:13.890
who may not know anything about migrants.

00:41:13.890 --> 00:41:18.360
They give them stereotypes
and negative things to repeat.

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:19.530
But they're doubly pernicious

00:41:19.530 --> 00:41:22.830
because for the community
that does know these people,

00:41:22.830 --> 00:41:26.280
they're coethnics, sometimes
literally they're relatives,

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:28.650
they make them feel like

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:31.080
they have to strategically reject them.

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:32.280
And again those are the people

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:34.470
that we might expect to be most welcoming,

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:37.412
but in this case they feel
pressured to reject them.

00:41:37.412 --> 00:41:39.843
So that's why I say
it's doubly pernicious.

00:41:40.890 --> 00:41:43.860
Next, public backlash to migrant shocks.

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:45.450
A lot of this backlash we've been seeing

00:41:45.450 --> 00:41:49.410
in Western countries is
not inevitable however.

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:52.230
If we can learn something
from Uganda, it could be that,

00:41:52.230 --> 00:41:54.360
host governments have agencies again

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:56.040
to shape that relationship

00:41:56.040 --> 00:41:57.930
and they can do various things

00:41:57.930 --> 00:42:01.173
to make it so that there's
less social conflict.

00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:05.370
Also I think in this project,

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:07.050
something that seems obvious,

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:10.525
but we should consider
is things like ethnicity,

00:42:10.525 --> 00:42:13.110
they're not just variables

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:15.510
to put on the right-hand side
of the regression, right?

00:42:15.510 --> 00:42:17.430
They are, in my case,

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:19.865
both an independent and
a dependent variable.

00:42:19.865 --> 00:42:22.920
We can feel more ethnic, less ethnic.

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:24.960
We can try and change
our ethnic identities

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:25.953
in different ways.

00:42:26.910 --> 00:42:30.030
And lastly, the book is going to end

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:32.880
considering on some other
cases where this might happen.

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:36.240
So for generalizability,
I'm collecting data,

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:39.090
focus group surveys right now

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:41.400
on the case of Venezuelan
migrants in Colombia,

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:43.792
again where we see a lot of coethnicity,

00:42:43.792 --> 00:42:45.990
Afghan refugees in Pakistan,

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:49.500
especially Afghan refugees
of Pashtun descent,

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:52.320
Pashtun minoritized groups in Pakistan,

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:55.320
and with Asian-Americans in the U.S.

00:42:55.320 --> 00:42:57.330
So I'm actually gonna do that
focus group in a few weeks

00:42:57.330 --> 00:43:00.033
and I'm very scared but excited.

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:03.120
Okay great, thank you so much.

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:03.953
Thanks for your questions.

00:43:03.953 --> 00:43:07.000
(audience applauding)

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:10.823
- [Audience Member] So I'm thinking

00:43:10.823 --> 00:43:14.490
about Hakeem Jefferson at
Stanford has this paper

00:43:14.490 --> 00:43:16.452
about the politics of respectability.

00:43:16.452 --> 00:43:18.000
It looks like you're familiar with it.

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:21.780
But what he's trying to explain
is why African-Americans

00:43:21.780 --> 00:43:25.020
have a high rate of supporting

00:43:25.020 --> 00:43:27.030
like pretty like tough on crime policies,

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:28.230
which seems like it doesn't make sense

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:30.750
because it disproportionately
affects their own group.

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:32.130
And the idea is that they want

00:43:32.130 --> 00:43:34.067
to separate themselves from like,

00:43:34.067 --> 00:43:36.363
good African-Americans
wanna separate themselves

00:43:36.363 --> 00:43:37.890
from the bad kind.

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:38.723
- Right.
- And they wanna like signal,

00:43:38.723 --> 00:43:39.556
they want some way for people

00:43:39.556 --> 00:43:42.883
to actually discriminate
bad and good ones.

00:43:42.883 --> 00:43:43.716
- Right?

00:43:43.716 --> 00:43:44.549
- [Audience Member] And I think

00:43:44.549 --> 00:43:45.480
there's like similar research looking

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:46.650
at immigrants in the U.S.

00:43:46.650 --> 00:43:47.891
having anti-immigrant attitudes

00:43:47.891 --> 00:43:48.854
and it's again
- Right.

00:43:48.854 --> 00:43:49.687
- [Audience Member] Trying to be like

00:43:49.687 --> 00:43:50.910
we're not like those kind.

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:51.743
- Exactly, yeah.
- Of immigrants.

00:43:51.743 --> 00:43:54.000
So do you think this is
kind of another instance

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:55.110
of that same phenomena

00:43:55.110 --> 00:43:57.180
of like trying to differentiate yourself

00:43:57.180 --> 00:43:58.650
to avoid discrimination

00:43:58.650 --> 00:44:00.360
and trying to like signal
that you are different?

00:44:00.360 --> 00:44:01.360
Is it kind of the same?

00:44:01.360 --> 00:44:02.875
- Yeah, I think so.

00:44:02.875 --> 00:44:06.510
I know I probably would feel the pressure

00:44:06.510 --> 00:44:07.830
to say, no, it's completely different.

00:44:07.830 --> 00:44:09.540
But, no, I think it is very similar.

00:44:09.540 --> 00:44:11.913
It's all built on social identity theory.

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:15.330
Yeah, Hakeem and I chatted a
few weeks ago and we're like,

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:17.878
yeah, we can kinda see
this phenomenon everywhere

00:44:17.878 --> 00:44:21.570
of if there's a group that's
going to make me look bad,

00:44:21.570 --> 00:44:24.060
make my family look bad,

00:44:24.060 --> 00:44:26.670
if I might be lumped in with that group,

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:28.893
then what can I do to separate us?

00:44:29.970 --> 00:44:32.700
And I think what he and
I were talking about

00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:35.370
was that's also context-specific, right?

00:44:35.370 --> 00:44:37.290
So the survey on respectability,

00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:39.360
it kind of depends on who they think

00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:41.610
that they're maybe
answering this question to

00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:43.740
or who they think that
they're signaling to.

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:46.530
I think in that case they're signaling

00:44:46.530 --> 00:44:50.910
to other Americans, white Americans,

00:44:50.910 --> 00:44:52.500
but if they were put
in a different context,

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:55.230
they might not act that way, right?

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:57.813
So I think this is the same.

00:44:59.010 --> 00:45:01.320
And I can just show in one survey, right?

00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:04.860
Like just shifting how the
survey questions are worded

00:45:04.860 --> 00:45:06.573
is gonna shift how people react.

00:45:07.770 --> 00:45:09.120
- [Audience Member] And
just a related point,

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:11.440
does it need to be state
stigmatization of the minority

00:45:11.440 --> 00:45:13.890
as opposed to just general stigmatization?

00:45:13.890 --> 00:45:14.723
Like how do?

00:45:14.723 --> 00:45:15.556
- Yeah.

00:45:15.556 --> 00:45:17.580
- [Audience Member] View
public, just public opinion

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:18.694
versus the state's actions,

00:45:18.694 --> 00:45:20.610
'cause they're not always aligned.

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:24.110
- That is how I'm kind of
struggling with this book.

00:45:24.110 --> 00:45:26.370
So thank you for picking out the part

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:31.370
that I think is tough because in my story,

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:33.750
it's very much like a top-down,

00:45:33.750 --> 00:45:37.530
like public opinion
follows elite rhetoric.

00:45:37.530 --> 00:45:39.990
It's kind of like that's
all the rest kind of world.

00:45:39.990 --> 00:45:43.200
And I think it's justifiable in the cases.

00:45:43.200 --> 00:45:46.260
Tanzania and Uganda are authoritarian,

00:45:46.260 --> 00:45:47.850
Kenya is more democratic,

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:50.005
but still I would say
especially on an issue

00:45:50.005 --> 00:45:52.830
that the state really cares about,

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:56.520
but if refugees are just in the camps,

00:45:56.520 --> 00:46:00.390
it's not like the public already
has xenophobic attitudes,

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:02.010
I think without state rhetoric.

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:03.630
I think in that case it's justified

00:46:03.630 --> 00:46:04.770
that it's sort of top down,

00:46:04.770 --> 00:46:08.070
public opinion follows elite rhetoric.

00:46:08.070 --> 00:46:10.113
But that's not true everywhere.

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:13.320
And so for example,

00:46:13.320 --> 00:46:16.200
Sandra Rosa's doing some
really cool work in Colombia,

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:18.125
I think you're on, are you
on some of those projects?

00:46:18.125 --> 00:46:20.594
- [Audience Member] We
were during the pandemic.

00:46:20.594 --> 00:46:21.690
- Oh gosh, (Yang-Yang laughs) okay.

00:46:21.690 --> 00:46:24.870
Yeah, but she's finding that in that case

00:46:24.870 --> 00:46:27.150
what's really interesting
is the Colombian state

00:46:27.150 --> 00:46:30.240
has all these like very generous policies

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:34.110
towards Venezuelans,
helping them regularize,

00:46:34.110 --> 00:46:36.570
letting them stay there
and work for like 10 years,

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:38.220
giving them access to services.

00:46:38.220 --> 00:46:40.290
And yet public opinion has really

00:46:40.290 --> 00:46:42.720
had a backlash towards those policies.

00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:45.810
And so Colombian citizens
have actually been

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:50.810
really negative about
it, and that seems weird.

00:46:50.820 --> 00:46:51.810
- [Audience Member] Yeah, I think.

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:52.643
- Yeah.

00:46:52.643 --> 00:46:54.313
- [Audience Member] What happens as well

00:46:54.313 --> 00:46:56.940
is some of the local politicians--

00:46:56.940 --> 00:46:58.110
- Right.
- I was gonna say,

00:46:58.110 --> 00:46:59.640
because in general,

00:46:59.640 --> 00:47:04.230
I mean the Venezuelan
refugees in Colombia,

00:47:04.230 --> 00:47:06.480
they have been accepted,
they don't live in camps,

00:47:06.480 --> 00:47:08.761
they have been given the right to work

00:47:08.761 --> 00:47:10.590
especially during the pandemic

00:47:10.590 --> 00:47:14.478
'cause they were also
given access to health.

00:47:14.478 --> 00:47:18.873
But the question, I mean the
comment there is more like,

00:47:19.980 --> 00:47:23.717
I mean some xenophobic and more populist--

00:47:23.717 --> 00:47:24.810
- Right.

00:47:24.810 --> 00:47:26.790
- [Audience Member] Comments
from some of the politician.

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:27.780
I think that's what

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:29.005
sparked as well.
- Like left-wing politicians.

00:47:29.005 --> 00:47:30.270
- Yeah.

00:47:30.270 --> 00:47:32.073
- [Audience Member] That
sparks the negative sentiment.

00:47:32.073 --> 00:47:34.530
And if they just said that,

00:47:34.530 --> 00:47:37.320
I mean the criminals are the Venezuelans

00:47:37.320 --> 00:47:38.880
and this is what happened

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:43.770
and then they amplify whatever
incident that has happened

00:47:43.770 --> 00:47:47.040
that then generalized to the other part

00:47:47.040 --> 00:47:48.870
that these are all the Venezuelan.

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:53.100
That's what probably you
managed to shift the opinion,

00:47:53.100 --> 00:47:55.575
because originally actually
the government of Colombia

00:47:55.575 --> 00:47:59.100
was not gonna give them access to health

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:01.110
and therefore the vaccines,

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:03.870
but there was actually a
backlash from the people.

00:48:03.870 --> 00:48:05.640
The Colombians say it should be

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:07.240
and then they switched that, so.

00:48:08.418 --> 00:48:09.251
- Yeah.

00:48:09.251 --> 00:48:11.430
- [Audience Member] So maybe
you might want to nuances

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:16.200
with some of the locals
and then maybe politicians

00:48:16.200 --> 00:48:18.690
and then nuance them in their rhetoric

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:21.780
of just general populism that they have.

00:48:21.780 --> 00:48:22.920
- That's great, thank you.

00:48:22.920 --> 00:48:24.900
I mean, I will say what we've,

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:26.730
so I've done some surveys

00:48:26.730 --> 00:48:31.730
with Maggie Peters and
Alicia Holland on Colombians.

00:48:32.310 --> 00:48:33.690
Specifically we did a survey

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:36.480
where we target Cucuta and Cali.

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:38.700
Cucuta's the border city,

00:48:38.700 --> 00:48:41.440
and so we think, we thought going in like,

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:44.550
because there's a lot of history
of like circular migration,

00:48:44.550 --> 00:48:47.790
just migrating for work every
day, that sort of thing,

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:49.770
that the expectation is that

00:48:49.770 --> 00:48:52.590
they would be more
welcoming of Venezuelans,

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:54.810
versus in Cali there's not as many there

00:48:54.810 --> 00:48:56.370
and so they might not know,

00:48:56.370 --> 00:48:57.810
they might not feel the connection,

00:48:57.810 --> 00:49:01.380
and they would be more
anti-Venezuelan migrants

00:49:01.380 --> 00:49:02.790
and anti these policies.

00:49:02.790 --> 00:49:04.020
But we actually found the opposite.

00:49:04.020 --> 00:49:07.320
In Cucuta, people were more likely to say

00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:11.400
that Venezuelans will
not integrate well here,

00:49:11.400 --> 00:49:13.020
that there's a big cultural difference

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:14.880
between Colombians and Venezuelans,

00:49:14.880 --> 00:49:16.710
and like a whole bunch of other things.

00:49:16.710 --> 00:49:18.480
So that sort of surprised us.

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:19.830
But I was also like, oh,

00:49:19.830 --> 00:49:21.870
well, maybe that sort of fits in here

00:49:21.870 --> 00:49:24.360
that other Colombians
when they think of Cucuta,

00:49:24.360 --> 00:49:26.109
they also think, well, they're basically,

00:49:26.109 --> 00:49:27.690
they could be Venezuelan.

00:49:27.690 --> 00:49:29.880
Like there's so much
cross-border migration.

00:49:29.880 --> 00:49:33.998
So maybe they face sort of
similar identity pressures.

00:49:33.998 --> 00:49:34.831
Yeah.

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:38.520
- [Man] Yeah, I happen
to be an active volunteer

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:41.490
in a refugee resettlement
group in New Haven.

00:49:41.490 --> 00:49:43.800
That's a Jewish ethnic group

00:49:43.800 --> 00:49:48.750
that's settling many
Muslim ethnic group peoples

00:49:48.750 --> 00:49:51.540
from Syria and Afghanistan.

00:49:51.540 --> 00:49:55.080
And the observation that I
have related to controversy

00:49:55.080 --> 00:49:59.460
is the fact that after the
refugees get comfortable

00:49:59.460 --> 00:50:02.103
by being settled by a Jewish American,

00:50:03.240 --> 00:50:05.700
and they see nice things happening,

00:50:05.700 --> 00:50:07.927
they finally say, "Oh my God,

00:50:07.927 --> 00:50:11.977
"I didn't know what to
expect to be resettled

00:50:11.977 --> 00:50:13.807
"or helped by a Jewish-American.

00:50:13.807 --> 00:50:15.277
"I thought it was gonna be terrible

00:50:15.277 --> 00:50:17.460
"because that's what I knew before."

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:18.871
And vice versa could have happened

00:50:18.871 --> 00:50:22.057
that the Jewish volunteers
could have said,

00:50:22.057 --> 00:50:24.367
"Well, with all the
propaganda we hear about

00:50:24.367 --> 00:50:25.597
"the Middle East and Israel,

00:50:25.597 --> 00:50:27.720
"that it would have gone the other way."

00:50:27.720 --> 00:50:32.040
So that in fact your preexisting
conditions are really there

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:34.240
and it invalidates
everything you're saying.

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:39.063
- Yeah, thank you so much
for that observation.

00:50:39.990 --> 00:50:43.350
Yeah, I mean basically, I
think what you're looking at

00:50:43.350 --> 00:50:45.510
is two groups that might not necessarily

00:50:45.510 --> 00:50:48.210
know about each other that much before,

00:50:48.210 --> 00:50:50.493
but through the sort of positive contact,

00:50:51.330 --> 00:50:55.433
they're learning to change misperceptions

00:50:55.433 --> 00:50:58.050
that they might have
held against each other.

00:50:58.050 --> 00:51:01.110
And I think it's interesting that

00:51:01.110 --> 00:51:03.153
put into a different context.

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:06.060
like here in New Haven,

00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:08.150
that contextual change can also change

00:51:08.150 --> 00:51:09.879
how they think about their own identities

00:51:09.879 --> 00:51:12.303
and other people's identities.

00:51:13.740 --> 00:51:16.710
If you wanna learn more
about contact theory work,

00:51:16.710 --> 00:51:20.373
I would redirect you to
Salma Mousa's research.

00:51:23.967 --> 00:51:26.100
